#!/bin/sh
# guards.config.sh — the node-ts ADAPTER's worked example.
#
# A filled-in replacement for the kit's `scripts/guards.config.sh`, for a pnpm
# workspace of TypeScript packages and apps with Vitest as the unit runner.
#
#   cp adapters/node-ts/guards.config.sh.example scripts/guards.config.sh
#
# COPY ONLY IF YOUR LAYOUT MATCHES. Every pattern below is a real value from the
# repo this framework was extracted from. Pasted into a repo of a different
# shape it produces a guard that measures nothing while looking configured,
# which is strictly worse than the unconfigured default (that one at least
# prints a warning on every push).
#
# The reasoning for each value is in adapters/node-ts/README.md §1 — read that
# before editing, not after.
#
# This file is DATA, not mechanism. The guards hold no knowledge of your layout;
# it all lives here, in one reviewable file, so a change of policy is a diff a
# human reads rather than an edit inside a script nobody re-reads.
#
# Sourced by: scripts/tdd-pairing-guard.sh (and its CI twin), and
#             scripts/behavior-delta.sh.
#
# THIS FILE IS YOURS once copied. It is not part of the shared layer (see
# VERSION) and no kit update overwrites it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. SOURCE — the production files whose change demands a test change
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# An extended regular expression (grep -E) matched against repo-relative paths.
#
# KEEP THIS IN STEP WITH `include` IN vitest.config.ts. Nothing ties the two
# together mechanically: a tree the runner covers but this pattern misses is
# silently un-guarded, and that is the one failure mode that produces a green
# push over untested code. Put a comment in vitest.config.ts pointing back here.
#
#   packages/<name>/src/           the workspace libraries
#   apps/<name>/src/               deployables
#   apps/<name>/app/(server|edit)/ the framework-routed tree, narrowed to the
#                                  two directories that hold behaviour — routes,
#                                  layouts and page components in the same tree
#                                  are covered by the browser tier, not this one
#   scripts/docs-conformance/      the docs gate's own validators, which run
#                                  under `node --test`. A repo that guards its
#                                  product but not its guards has a hole exactly
#                                  where it matters most.
#
# `[^/]+` and not `.*`: an anchored single segment cannot be satisfied by a
# nested `packages/x/node_modules/y/src/`.
#
# NOT included, deliberately: `scripts/*.sh`. Widening to shell would capture
# every other script in the repo, most of which have no test tier — a rule
# expansion with its own blast radius rather than a gap to patch in passing.
# What covers the guards is their own suite (tests/tdd-pairing-guard*.test.sh).
GUARD_SOURCE_RE='^(packages/[^/]+/src/|apps/[^/]+/src/|apps/[^/]+/app/(server|edit)/|scripts/docs-conformance/).*\.(ts|tsx|mjs)$'

# Paths that match SOURCE but must NOT count as source.
#
# Test files are NOT listed here on purpose: the guard already subtracts
# GUARD_TEST_RE from the source list before asking the pairing question, so a
# copy of that rule here would be a second definition free to drift.
#
#   *.d.ts / *.min.js   declarations and build output — no behaviour to test
#   index.ts            barrel files: pure re-exports. A barrel that gains a
#                       line has moved no behaviour (stryker.config.mjs excludes
#                       them for the same reason)
#   docs-conformance/config.mjs
#                       reviewable policy DATA, not mechanism. A file that is
#                       READ rather than executed changes by human decision, not
#                       by new behaviour, so editing it demands no test edit.
#
# Add your own config-as-data files here ONE AT A TIME, each with its reason. An
# exclusion list that grows without reasons is how a guard gets switched off in
# instalments.
GUARD_SOURCE_EXCLUDE_RE='\.d\.ts$|\.min\.js$|(^|/)index\.ts$|^scripts/docs-conformance/config\.mjs$'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. TEST — what counts as the paired test change
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Narrower than the kit's cross-ecosystem default, because this repo has exactly
# two conventions: the Vitest suffix and Gherkin.
#
# Direction of error matters. A too-BROAD test pattern makes the guard more
# forgiving and never more aggressive; a too-narrow one blocks honest pushes and
# gets the guard deleted, and a deleted guard checks nothing. When unsure, err
# broad and narrow it once you know what your repo actually names things.
GUARD_TEST_RE='\.(test|spec)\.(ts|tsx|mjs)$|\.feature$'

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. CONTRACT ARTIFACTS — the surfaces behavior-delta.sh inventories
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# One record per line: a human-readable label, a pipe, then one or more extended
# regexes. behavior-delta.sh prints a section per surface with deltas on the
# branch, and the SAME set drives its per-commit `refactor:`-that-is-not check.
#
# A contract artifact is a place where behavior is EXTERNALIZED and therefore
# machine-visible. The test for a candidate path is one question: IF THIS FILE
# CHANGES AND NOBODY LOOKS, WHO FINDS OUT AND HOW? "A consumer, in production"
# means contract artifact. "The type-checker, in twelve seconds" does not.
#
# Keep the list SHORT. A human reads this report on every review; a surface list
# matching half the repo produces a report nobody reads, which is worse than no
# report because it looks like coverage.
#
# The first two records are the kit's own defaults and hold for any project
# built on it. The six below them are this adapter's:
#
#   API surface     the document a consumer codes against — a change here is a
#                   change to someone else's build. The SPEC, not the handler;
#                   handlers are ordinary source the pairing guard covers.
#   Persistence     a migration is behavior that runs once, in production, and
#                   is not undone by reverting the commit. Both the ORM schema
#                   and a raw migrations/ tree, because projects acquire the
#                   second the moment the first cannot express something.
#   Env contract    a new REQUIRED variable is a deployment change disguised as
#                   a one-line diff: it compiles, CI has the value, the deploy
#                   fails.
#   HTTP posture    a CSP directive or a SameSite flag is security behavior with
#                   no test that naturally fails when it is wrong — exactly the
#                   profile of a change a human must confirm (invariant §5).
#   Error semantics status code and body shape are API contract even though they
#                   live in a library. Clients branch on them.
#   Prompt surfaces if the product's surface IS prompt text, editing that text
#                   is editing behavior and the compiler will never say so.
BEHAVIOR_DELTA_SURFACES='Agent & process surfaces (the constitution, skills, hooks, guards)|^AGENTS\.md$|/AGENTS\.md$|^CLAUDE\.md$|^GEMINI\.md$|^constitution/|^\.claude/|^\.githooks/|^scripts/
Architecture decisions|^docs/adr/
API surface (OpenAPI / GraphQL / protobuf)|^docs/api/openapi\.yaml$|\.graphql$|\.proto$
Persistence (schema + migrations)|^packages/db/|^prisma/schema\.prisma$|^migrations/|^docs/db-design\.md$
Configuration (the env contract)|^packages/env/|\.env\.example$
HTTP posture (headers, CSP, CORS)|^packages/headers/|^apps/[^/]+/middleware\.ts$
Error semantics (the shared error model)|^packages/http/
Agent-facing prompt surfaces|^apps/mcp/(src/(instructions|prompts|tools)|skill/)'

# Executable specification files. behavior-delta flags these when EDITED inside
# a structure-only commit: an edited scenario is an unambiguous behavior change,
# while a newly added one is just added coverage.
BEHAVIOR_DELTA_FEATURE_RE='\.feature$'
